00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.060 align:start position:0%
 
Cecile Richards is entering her final  weeks at the helm of Planned Parenthood  the organization provides health care  programs like birth control cancer  screening pregnancy care and abortion  services to more than 2 million people  at more than 600 centers nationwide for  12 years Richards fought for women's  health care and organized campaigns to  protect Planned Parenthood's federal and  state funding  she will step down as president next  month she shares her own story for the  first time in her new book it's called  make trouble standing up and speaking  out and finding the courage to leave to  lead rather you could say leave this we  go it's published by touchstone that's  an imprint of Simon &amp; Schuster a  division of CBS Cecile Richards good  morning to you good to see you good  morning it's been a twelve years how  would you describe it as you as you're  settling into your last week and why now  do you think it's time to go well it's  an incredible organization of course we  just celebrated our 100th anniversary  and it's been an enormous honor we  provide health care to one in five women  in this country and so it's hard to  leave but I'm ready to step aside and  have someone else take on their  responsibilities at a time where I think  we're incredibly strong in this in this  country and I think it's important as  you point out you provide health care  but for so many people Cecile they hear  Planned Parenthood and it's like this  it's very polarizing and very divisive  for a lot of people because they only  concentrate on the word abortion what do  you say about that well I mean again I  think we do more to prevent unintended  pregnancy than any organization in this  country and we always have it was  interesting Gail I just saw Fox News  poll out last week they did with the  Wall Street Journal that plan paired it  was the most popular organization in the  country of the groups that they polled  so for most women in this country and a  lot of men planned parent actually isn't  a problem it's more of a solution  you said it's not a political issue you  said it's a health care issue that's  correct I mean folks don't come to us  with to make a political statement they  come because they want access to  affordable health care and we're proud  to provide it all across America and  more than 600 health centers and federal  funding is so important for the  organization the president in his most  recent budget said never again he was  reluctant to sign that one of the  reasons obviously it was for funding for  organizations like Planned Parenthood  that was included in this recent budget  but there are concerns that that won't  be included in  budgets to come are you concerned about  that as you hand over the reins actually  because of the outpouring of support of  people across the country many of them  former and current Planned Parenthood  patients I feel really good about where  we are and we had bipartisan support in  the United States Senate that kept our  doors open last year and is again I  think as more and more folks have  focused on what it would mean when you  talk about defunding Planned period it  really means blocking women from getting  birth control cancer screenings and for  many women  we're the only health care provider that  they have you tell an interesting story  about meeting with Jared and Ivanka  early on in the after the election to  talk about plan I thought that was a  very interesting conversation will you  share that sure well and you can read  more in the book I think it's kind of a  behind-the-scenes look at you know they  I'd heard that they were interested in  meeting to learn more about Planned  Parenthood and I would do anything go  anywhere talk to anyone about the  important care that we provide so I took  my husband actually because I wanted  somebody else with me and we we sat and  talked to them but it really seemed  pretty clear that they just wanted to  make a political deal they and wanted us  to quit providing abortion services to  women in America didn't they say we  could guarantee funding if you say  you'll you'll stop you'll stop abortion  that's basically what they said and I  just said we will never turn our backs  on women in America it's a legal service  and Plus as you say no federal funding  goes for abortion services so though I  was very disappointed in the outcome of  the meeting I have been incredibly  encouraged again at the outpouring of  people who support Planned Parenthood  and that's really why our doors have  stayed open this last year what do you  make of the polarization around abortion  rights in politics today I mean  Democrats there used to be pro-life  democrats in the Democratic Party right  now you cannot be against abortion  rights and survive as a Democrat that's  happened over time why do you think that  it I actually don't think that's right I  think that the there's a difference of  people in America see which is the game  of their own feelings personal feelings  about abortion and it is a very personal  topic and I certainly respect that but  it's different to say that you want the  government to tell women and pregnant  people what they can do and that's where  I think actually there is overwhelming  support now for Roe versus Wade the  highest that there's ever been in this  kind  including among Republicans so because  it's the difference between respecting  people's personal opinion and what do  you want the government to actually do  but do you think if you're a Democrat  who agrees on restrictions of abortion  of some kind that you will have success  in the Democratic Party well I mean I  don't know there are people running all  across the country I will tell you one  thing that has been encouraging this  year and I write about it in my book is  the record number of women running for  office I have never seen the outpouring  of women being not only candidates but  activists I think it's an exciting time  frankly I think of and if we got finally  got half of Congress could get pregnant  we would quit fighting about birth  control and planned parent and we'd move  on to some other issues well I think  it's so appropriate that the name of  your book is called make trouble because  you talked about as a little kid you  refused in class to say the Lord's  Prayer and then later in seventh grade  you wore a black armband to protest the  Vietnam War and we're called to the  principal's office right that's been  kind of my history of course I talk  about in the book as well growing up  with like Ann Richards as my mother's  mother yeah who went on to become the  first progressive woman governor ever in  the state of Texas talk a little bit  about what was like raising activist  kids but it's like being pregnant with  twins and trying to go on the campaign  trail when there's no more maternity  clothes that could possibly fit you so I  hope this book is both a memoir but also  a little bit of a call to action for  people who want to make a difference in  the world but I was fortunate enough to  meet your mother when she was governor  of my home state of Texas and I'm  curious she was never one to hold back  her thoughts right and she spoke her  mind what would she think about the  current political landscape right now in  this country I think what mom would feel  is the incredibly excited about the  number of women who are now standing up  speaking out whether it's me too whether  it's time's up whether it is women  running for office and I know we've seen  that and one of the things I'll write  about in my book is the amazing women  including many young women I've met  along the trail this year and she'd  really be proud of her her kids or  grandkids my daughter Lily Adams who now  works for Kamala Harris a fantastic  United States Senator and the world is  changing I think women are shifting the  shifting the ground everywhere and mom  would love that  women running for office does that mean  Cecile Richards running for office  well maybe Never Say Never but I'm  really excited in these next few months  to make sure  that women are not only running for  office but they are registered to vote  and that they're turning out in November  I think women have the opportunity to  change the landscape and change the  direction of America  all right make trouble yeah and you said  make trouble never ends troublemaking  never ends you say that's right  interesting to see your next chapter  thank you Thank You Gayle thanks sir  yeah good to see you thank you Richards  make trouble is on sale tomorrow

